# Logging into a past date requires its plan file to exist
! plan @~1 -m "yesterday note"
# Printing a missing plan exits 2 silently
! plan yesterday
# A lone argument must be a selector; a bare message gets a hint
! plan "just a message"
# The removed subcommands point at their replacements
! plan log "old habit"
! plan jot "old habit"
! plan show
-- cmd_1_exit.txt --
2
-- cmd_1_stderr.txt --
plan: No plan file for that date: 2026-02-18.plan
-- cmd_2_exit.txt --
2
-- cmd_3_exit.txt --
2
-- cmd_3_stderr.txt --
plan: Invalid selector. Use a day (@, @~N, @-, today, yesterday, 'N days ago', 2026-11-01), a set of plans (11-01, 01, 2026-11, 2026), or a range (@~6.., 2026-10..2026-11, ..). To log a message, use -m: plan -m "just a message"
-- cmd_4_exit.txt --
2
-- cmd_4_stderr.txt --
plan: `plan log` is now `plan [selector] -m <message>`, e.g. `plan -m "ship it"`
-- cmd_5_exit.txt --
2
-- cmd_5_stderr.txt --
plan: `plan log` is now `plan [selector] -m <message>`, e.g. `plan -m "ship it"`
-- cmd_6_exit.txt --
2
-- cmd_6_stderr.txt --
plan: `plan show` is now `plan <selector>` with stdout piped, e.g. `plan yesterday | bat`
